WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.026 --> 00:00:03.280
- For two years, this drug was a miracle.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.480
It was introduced in 1996 to treat HIV.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.320
And by 1998, 75,000
patients across the country

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.960
were taking up to 20 of them every day.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.560
It's called ritonavir,

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.040
and it turned a certain death
into a manageable condition.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.880
This particular pill is on its way

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.840
to quality control to
a dissolution tester.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.120
Here, analysts monitor
each batch of capsules,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.680
checking that they do
dissolve in around 30 minutes,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.400
which is quickly enough
to be absorbed properly.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.400
It's a rigorous precaution for a drug

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.720
that for two years and
240 consecutive lots

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.120
has never failed.
(grim music)

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.480
But now an analyst sees something unusual.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.680
This capsule hasn't dissolved properly.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.600
So they follow protocol and
trigger an emergency shutdown.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.320
(alarm blaring)

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.320
They destroy the entire batch

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.000
and deep clean the production line

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.760
to eliminate any possible
traces of contamination.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.920
But the next day, at quality control,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.600
the same thing happens.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.600
On the line the clear capsules
are turning white and cloudy,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.400
technicians at the nearby
research and development lab

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.720
study the paste under a microscope

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.480
and find they're filled with
millions of tiny needles.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.920
They're crystals, but no
one has seen them before.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.120
They need a control to
compare the needles against,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.240
so they make some of their
own ritonavir in the lab,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.680
but to their horror, it
also comes out cloudy.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.320
So they try again, but all
attempts yield the same result:

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.360
a white paste every time.
(subdued music)

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.600
The researchers are stumped.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.960
They had been making
ritonavir for two years.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.360
They knew its exact chemical composition

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.480
and every part of the
process used to make it,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.520
so they check all the
input ingredients again,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.880
all the settings, every
temperature setting and procedure.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.760
But all of it seems to be done correctly.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:58.720
Yet at the factory,

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.480
the cloudy capsules are appearing
more and more frequently.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.720
Within a week, every tablet produced

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.400
by either the lab or the
factory comes out cloudy.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.480
Abbott needs to halt all production

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.880
of ritonavir immediately.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.720
But they can't just cut off the supply

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.440
because people need these tablets.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.320
- [Narrator] "We called on as
many resources as we could.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.760
We tried everything.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.760
We conducted countless experiments.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.840
We rebuilt facilities and new lines.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.760
We looked at alternative sites

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.064
to see if we could start
clean in a new environment."

00:02:32.064 --> 00:02:32.078
(gentle music)

00:02:32.078 --> 00:02:36.000
- And they found an alternative
site, a factory in Italy.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.920
They start ritonavir production there,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.280
and to their relief,

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.920
all the pills passed the dissolution test.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.760
This is great news.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.880
But it also means

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.920
that Chicago must have
been making a mistake.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.920
So a team of scientists flies over

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.640
to look at what the Italians
are doing differently.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.080
They check everything, the
pressure, temperature, humidity,

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.160
the exact weight of all the chemicals,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:02:59.760
but it all matches perfectly

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.080
with what they're doing in Chicago.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.000
None of it makes any sense.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.000
But at least Italy can
keep making the medicine.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:08.960
(phone ringing)
(soft brooding music)

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.480
But when the Chicago team
returns home, they get a call.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:13.920
It's from Italy.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.920
Within days after their visit,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.667
one of the tablets fails
the dissolution test.

00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:21.280
- [Narrator] "There was no gradual trend.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.320
There was no early warning.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.440
In a matter of weeks,
maybe five or six weeks,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.280
every place the product was

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.520
became contaminated with the crystals.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.120
We did not know how to detect it.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.720
We did not know how to test for it.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.720
We did not know what caused it.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.880
We did not know how to prevent it.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.880
We did not know how to get rid of it.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.200
And we kept asking the
question, 'Why now?'"

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.280
- They were witnessing a rare disaster.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.240
It had happened before, and
in theory could happen again

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.280
to just about any drug
or chemical compound.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.280
It spreads like a disease,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.640
but the thing that's getting
infected is the medicine.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.400
One day you can make it,
the next it's gone forever.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.320
- [Narrator] "It is frightening
that this could happen

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.920
to any drug that we've taken
on, which we're dependent."

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.640
- And the scariest part

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.560
is you can't predict if it will
happen, when it will happen,

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.200
or to which medicine or compound.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.160
Overnight, drugs we all rely
on might just disappear.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.760
So what was happening inside
those ritonavir capsules?

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.920
What were those crystals inside?

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.560
They appeared to be an
entirely new compound,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.080
but when they tested them,

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.200
everything indicated they were ritonavir.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:34.960
It sounds impossible,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.120
but something similar had
actually been the center

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.975
of a heated debate 170 years earlier.

00:04:41.975 --> 00:04:41.989
(mellow music)

00:04:41.989 --> 00:04:43.840
- [Casper] In his Paris laboratory,

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.120
chemist Justus von Liebig
was reading a paper.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.440
It was about a newly discovered compound,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.840
and what elements it was made of.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:52.640
This kind of work

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.120
was at the cutting edge
of chemical research.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.440
Research he knew better than almost anyone

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.640
because he had personally
pioneered most of it.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.200
This had made him highly
respected in his field,

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.960
but he also had a reputation

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.880
for being difficult to work with.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.480
He was arrogant, hot-tempered,
and didn't suffer fools.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.400
And the more he read this paper,

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.880
the more incensed he got, because to him,

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.160
it was clearly written by
a fool, Friedrich Wöhler.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.560
So we headed over to the lab at Imperial

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.400
to recreate what Wöhler
claimed to have discovered.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.360
- So I've got it here wrapped in foil

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.509
because it is a bit photo-sensitive.

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:31.800
- [Casper] It's a bit like,
like, little rocks in there.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:32.360
- Yeah.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.080
- Beige powder, okay.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.280
Made of one silver, one
nitrogen, one oxygen,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.949
and one carbon.
- Exactly.

00:05:38.949 --> 00:05:40.152
- (laughs) You wanna light it up?

00:05:40.152 --> 00:05:42.720
- Yeah, sure, let's do it.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:43.840
You seem quite excited.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.920
- I am quite excited.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.440
Yeah. Not much is happening.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.600
Oh, it's melting a little, or it's, like,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.264
it is getting a little discolored.

00:05:55.264 --> 00:05:56.720
- [Dr. Kafizas] Yes, it is.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.520
- I guess the issue was he said,

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.040
"Okay, I found this beige powder

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.800
and I know exactly what it's made of,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.760
one silver, one carbon, one
nitrogen, and one oxygen."

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:07.200
He publishes this.
(curious music)

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.160
The paper reaches Liebig, and he is like,

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.760
"There's no way, because I've
just discovered that compound.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.840
And when I tried to put a flame to it,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.160
it behaves completely differently."

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.265
And we've got some of that right here too.

00:06:21.265 --> 00:06:21.280
(serious music)

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.520
Should we try to burn this one?

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.720
- Yeah. I made some fresh this morning.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.880
Let's just see how a small amount behaves.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.880
So we're gonna go with, I don't know,

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.160
maybe a few milligrams.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.120
I've left it a bit moist.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.304
When it's in its moist-
(compound snaps)

00:06:36.304 --> 00:06:36.320
Oh!
- Oh, my God.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.073
I didn't expect that.
- Yeah!

00:06:38.073 --> 00:06:39.998
- It's so loud.
(Dr. Kafizas laughs)

00:06:39.998 --> 00:06:42.400
- It's very sensitive.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.224
I'm sorry about that.
- That is crazy.

00:06:44.224 --> 00:06:45.360
- [Dr. Kafizas] And that
was just a small amount.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.200
- Oh my God, my ears.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:48.800
What? I was not ready for that.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.480
- I wasn't ready for that either.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.560
I made it moist so that it's less likely

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.760
to self-detonate, but clearly I was wrong.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.360
- Clearly Wöhler had made a mistake.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.960
These can't possibly be made
of exactly the same elements.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.920
So Liebig wrote a paper
slamming Wöhler's work,

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:07.840
calling him a "hopeless analyst"

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.000
and saying he should go
back, check his work,

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.600
and publish again when
he's found his mistake.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.680
And Wöhler does exactly that.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.400
He checks his work but finds no mistakes.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.120
So now he's even more
sure that he's correct.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.160
So he writes up his
results in a second paper,

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.400
but Liebig wasn't having any of this

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.840
and replies with another
paper saying he must be wrong.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.120
So this public back-and-forth
continues for two years,

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.600
with each side becoming
more and more convinced

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.840
that the other is out of their mind,

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:39.440
until finally they agreed

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:41.360
to meet on neutral ground in Frankfurt

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.080
to put this whole thing
to bed once and for all.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.680
They would replicate each other's work

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.960
and let the results speak for themselves.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.400
But when they did, they were stunned.

