00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:43.586
And now back to what was
happening with Liebig and Wöhler.

00:09:43.586 --> 00:09:43.598
(soft music)

00:09:43.598 --> 00:09:44.880
- They were both right?
- Yeah.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.160
- Like, they both had a compound

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.880
that was made of exactly
one carbon, one nitrogen,

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:50.880
one oxygen, and one silver atom.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.840
And one could be boring as hell (laughs)

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:59.401
and the other one can,
well, blow up your face.

00:09:59.401 --> 00:09:59.422
(compound snaps)
- Oh!

00:09:59.422 --> 00:09:59.438
(compound bangs)

00:09:59.438 --> 00:10:01.918
- Whoa! (laughs)

00:10:01.918 --> 00:10:04.000
- This was surprising because at the time

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.480
a compound was thought
to be just the atoms

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.880
that made it up and nothing more.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.080
But now this whole
conception had to change.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:14.160
Von Liebig and Wöhler had discovered

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.080
that the way those atoms
are arranged also matters.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:21.040
At the time, they had no way
to work out that ordering,

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.501
but today we can.

00:10:23.501 --> 00:10:23.520
(inquisitive music)

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.600
When you shine light on a molecule,

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.120
its electric field tugs on
the electrons and nuclei.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:30.640
They get pulled back and forth

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.040
as the field changes direction.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:34.720
This can stretch, squeeze,

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:36.800
and bend the bonds in the molecule

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.440
as the atoms oscillate back and forth.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.680
But each bond responds
differently to the light

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.360
depending on how strong it is

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.920
and the mass of the atoms it connects.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:48.880
It's like each bond is
a boat on the ocean.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.120
If the waves are small and rapid,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.120
they won't rock it very much.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:54.880
And if the waves are very slow,

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.960
like the tides coming in and out,

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.520
that also won't rock the boat very much.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.000
The boat just gets lifted up and down.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.880
It's only when the waves
are just the right size

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.920
that the boat gets tossed around.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:10.000
And because of this, each
bond will react strongest

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.680
to a specific frequency of
light, which we can measure.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:14.960
By hitting the molecule

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.880
with a range of infrared frequencies,

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:18.720
we get a spectrum like this

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.920
with peaks that tell us
when bonds are reacting.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.160
This acts like a
fingerprint for the molecule

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.080
and tells us which bonds are there.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.240
Wöhler's compound has a broad peak,

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:31.760
which corresponds to bending

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:35.600
an N double-bonded to C
double-bonded to O group.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.200
Liebig's compound, on the other hand,

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.200
has a spectrum that looks like this,

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.440
with these two prominent peaks,

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:44.160
one at high frequency
and one at low frequency.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.160
These correspond to
stretching a double bond

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:47.840
between a carbon and a nitrogen

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:51.120
and a single bond between
a nitrogen and an oxygen.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.480
We now know that Wöhler's
compound was silver cyanate,

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.080
and it looks like this.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:58.240
The carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.120
are joined with those
two strong double bonds,

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:02.880
which is why it's so stable.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:06.000
In contrast, Liebig's
compound, silver fulminate,

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.200
looks like this.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.160
The silver is bonded
to the carbon instead,

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.120
so the other elements
are arranged this way.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:16.720
The bond between the carbon
and nitrogen is a triple bond,

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:20.120
but the oxygen and nitrogen
are very weakly connected.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:23.360
And this single bond
is very easy to break,

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.240
and once it does,

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.920
the atoms can rearrange
into much more stable gases,

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:30.320
which is why it's so explosive.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.400
They had discovered isomers.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.560
That it's not just the atoms in a molecule

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:38.960
that dictate how it behaves,
but its bonds as well.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:41.120
So naturally the scientists at Abbott

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:44.640
suspected something similar
might be happening to ritonavir.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.960
They knew that the spectrum of ritonavir

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:48.560
should look like this.

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:52.160
So they put a sample of white
paste into a spectrometer,

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:54.720
expecting to see something
completely different.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:59.440
But instead they saw this, the same peaks.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.880
The paste had all the
same bonds as ritonavir,

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.440
so it must be ritonavir.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.240
But they also noticed it
wasn't exactly the same.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.520
There were these small
deviations between the two.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.760
The arrangement of the atoms was the same,

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.000
but something about the
bonds had changed slightly.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.680
- Well, it turns out there's another way

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.560
to change the properties,
and I can show you how

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:25.840
with probably the most
delicious demo I'll ever do,

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:28.720
because this, of course,
is a piece of chocolate.

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:31.440
It's nice, it's shiny, it's durable,

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:34.240
and it has that nice
snap when you crack it.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:34.880
But you'll notice

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.680
that if you've ever let
your chocolate melt,

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.320
then it never returns
to being quite the same.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:43.120
Suddenly it melts in your
hand when you pick it up,

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:44.880
you know, it's dull, it's bendy,

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:46.960
and it doesn't quite taste the same.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:48.160
You're not imagining this.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.840
There really is a subtle difference,

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:51.280
and I can explain what's happening

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.920
with a little help from
my friend Chris over here.

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:54.960
- Hey.
- Hello.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.000
So Chris runs his own YouTube
channel, Chris Young Cooks,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.560
and this is way overkill for
what we need here probably,

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.240
because he was the head development chef

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.800
at a three-Michelin star restaurant.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:05.983
- Yes.

00:14:05.983 --> 00:14:05.998
(subdued music)

00:14:05.998 --> 00:14:08.560
- We've got some nice
shiny chocolate here.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:17.344
But look what happens
when we turn up the heat.

00:14:17.344 --> 00:14:17.360
(heat gun whirs)

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:23.040
Oh yeah, that goes quite quick.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:27.355
Oh, that is surprisingly satisfying.

00:14:27.355 --> 00:14:30.400
- We obviously melted some of
the chocolate, no surprise.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:31.440
But this is what happens, right?

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:32.560
You leave it somewhere warm,

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:34.240
the chocolate gets above body temperature,

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:36.400
it starts to melt, and
then as it cools back down,

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:37.360
it's gonna harden again.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.510
- So you've got the chocolate.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:41.669
- [Chris] That looks like
heat-damaged chocolate, right?

00:14:41.669 --> 00:14:42.000
- [Casper] I know.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.480
- Like, you've seen this,
you've opened a chocolate bar,

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:46.800
maybe it was left in your car
sitting in a sunny window.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:49.760
Touch the edge, like, you can feel,

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.518
feel how that's just soft-
- Yeah.

00:14:51.518 --> 00:14:52.800
- and kind of sticky.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:53.800
- Immediately.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.880
- Compare it to a nicely
tempered piece of chocolate.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.720
Like, you can pick that
up with your bare hands,

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:01.760
it will eventually melt in your
hand, but much more slowly.

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:04.480
- Now if this ever
accidentally happens to you,

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.000
we'll show you how to get it back

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.000
to the nice and shiny form.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.200
But what's interesting here

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:12.160
is that we didn't change
any of the ingredients

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.280
and yet the properties changed completely.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.920
Chocolate is made of
three main ingredients.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.440
There are other minor ones as well,

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.280
but three main ones to focus on.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:24.880
It's got cocoa solids, that's
what gives it its color,

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:26.960
there are sugar, of course, for sweetness,

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:28.880
and then there is cocoa butter.

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:31.186
That's what gives it its texture.

00:15:31.186 --> 00:15:31.200
(gentle music)

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.280
And this cocoa butter is the culprit.

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:35.920
It's a fat made from
three long carbon chains

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:39.600
bonded together in the middle
to make this sort of Y shape.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:42.880
And that Y shape can form
together to form solids.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.520
But there are multiple ways
they can stack together.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.000
There are many forms the crystal can take,

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.680
each with different properties.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:52.640
And so we call these polymorphs.

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:55.440
Chocolate actually has six polymorphs.

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.920
The dull chocolate is mostly Form IV,

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:01.600
and that has a melting point
of around 27 degrees Celsius.

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:04.400
While the shiny chocolate,
which is the one we want,

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:07.600
is mostly Form V, and that
has a higher melting point

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:09.758
of around 34 degrees Celsius.

00:16:09.758 --> 00:16:12.880
- So the challenge and the
art of chocolate making

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:14.560
is managing these polymorphs

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:16.320
to get the right form of crystal

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.360
by managing both temperature
and, importantly, time.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:21.440
The nice thing about chocolate
is you can start over.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.160
You just need to heat it
back up to 45 to 50 Celsius

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:25.598
to wipe the memory-
- Okay.

00:16:25.598 --> 00:16:26.640
- of the wrong crystals.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.200
That's hot enough to melt
out all of the crystals,

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:31.760
but not too hot to start changing

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:32.720
the flavor of the chocolate,

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:35.109
evaporating a lot of
the volatile aromatics.

00:16:35.109 --> 00:16:36.480
- [Casper] After around 10 minutes

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:38.400
at roughly 50 degrees Celsius,

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.480
all the crystals should have fully melted.

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:43.440
- So at this point, we're
trying to cool it back down

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.080
to the temperature where
crystals start to form again.

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:48.640
And that's gonna start
at about 34 Celsius.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.520
You'll start getting Form
V crystals forming at 34,

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:52.720
as we cool even lower,

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:55.120
we start to get Form IV and Form III.

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:57.360
Those can all form at these temperatures.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.000
And that's okay. We want all
of these crystals initially.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:00.680
We want-
- Oh, really?

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:02.480
- Yeah, we want to have

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.360
sort of a shotgun of nucleation going on

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:08.360
because we wanna make sure
we get lots of everything.

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:09.400
- That's surprising.

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:10.760
- It does seem surprising.

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:13.200
- Because we just want Form V, right?

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:14.880
- We do just want Form V.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.520
The trick is, if we just
come down to the temperature

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.280
where Form V forms,

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:22.400
so if we just went to like
32 degrees, 33 degrees,

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.880
and just waited there, you'd
be waiting a very long time

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:26.880
and you'd get a very random process

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.720
of when does that crystal form,

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:30.560
and maybe only a few crystals would form.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.073
And so they would get very large.

00:17:32.073 --> 00:17:33.280
- Ah.
- By bringing the temperature

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:34.720
all the way down to 27,

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.800
we get lots of nucleation really fast.

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:37.600
The downside, of course,

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:40.160
is we get the crystals
we don't want as well,

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:41.600
but we get lots of Form V,

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:43.280
and we get lots of small Form V.

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:43.680
- Yeah.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.240
- So once we have that starting to form,

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.000
we can select for the ones we want

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.480
just by raising the temperature back up.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.880
And melting the Form III and Form IV,

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.280
leaving us with only
Form V, but, importantly,

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.440
lots of Form V.
- Right.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:58.720
After holding the chocolate

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:02.160
at around 32 degrees
Celsius for 5 to 10 minutes,

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:03.840
we can pour it into the mold.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:07.800
- Okay, I think we're
gonna be okay here, so.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:10.480
- Oh.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:11.680
I don't know what I was expecting,

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:13.600
but I was not expecting
it to go like this.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.560
It really comes out as a sort of sheet.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.800
- One of the things here

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.800
is I do have some trapped air bubbles.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:26.020
- Yep.

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:28.520
- Oh, yeah.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:32.933
- It's like a liquefaction.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:32.933 --> 00:18:35.360
- [Chris] So at this point,

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:36.960
seems like we're done, right?
- Yeah.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:38.720
- But actually now we need to lock in

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:41.192
that crystal pattern that
we've created, right?

00:18:41.192 --> 00:18:42.480
- Yep.
- Like, as it cools down,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:43.520
there's liquid oil in there

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:45.040
and we're gonna drop back
down through the temperature

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.512
where Form IV and Form III can form.

00:18:47.512 --> 00:18:48.320
- Yep.
- So what we need to do

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:49.600
is we need to come down

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:51.440
through that temperature
relatively quickly

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:54.240
so that we get mostly Form V growing

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:57.440
and lock them in by getting
rid of most of the liquid oil.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:00.640
So we really need to get
this down to about 12 C.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:02.080
- [Casper] So we put it in the fridge

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.480
and waited for around 20 to 30 minutes.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:08.640
- Get the door closed.
- Great.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:12.000
If we did this correctly,
it should be mostly Form V,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.600
which means all the molecules

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:15.760
should have stacked tightly together,

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:18.861
resulting in a shiny and snappy bar.

00:19:18.861 --> 00:19:20.880
(tray cracking)
Ah! Ooh!

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:21.800
That was satisfying.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.680
- They're just barely hanging on.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:24.460
- Wow!

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:26.960
- And you can see they're nice.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:28.200
- Perfect.
- Shiny.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:29.840
- This is all Form V?

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:35.280
- This is all Form V, and
we've got a nice shiny surface.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:36.880
Got a couple spots where maybe the molds

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:38.320
could have been polished
a little bit more,

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:40.941
but give it a snap, just see how that is.

00:19:40.941 --> 00:19:42.390
(chocolate snaps)
- Ooh. It's a very good snap.

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:43.120
- [Chris] Yeah, that's a nice.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:44.960
- It's very sturdy.

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:48.062
- That's a good chocolate bar.

00:19:48.062 --> 00:19:48.080
- Delicious.
- That

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:49.680
is how you temper a chocolate bar.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:51.118
- Amazing.
(curious music)

00:19:51.118 --> 00:19:53.280
- But the stacking of the
molecules in the crystals

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.200
also changes something else.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:58.240
Since each molecule is
surrounded by other molecules,

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.360
it changes how the bonds inside can move.

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:03.120
This is what the scientists at Abbott

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.720
had seen in the spectrum.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:06.880
The needles they had
seen under the microscope

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:09.760
were a new polymorph of ritonavir,

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.920
and a more stable one at that.

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:15.280
Form I crystals looked like this instead.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.600
Now at first this might
seem like good news.

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:21.120
It was still ritonavir, even
if it looked a bit different.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:23.120
It's just like how dull chocolate,

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:26.800
even if it's not quite as
nice, is still chocolate.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:30.800
But the problem was this new
polymorph was far too stable.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.680
- Ritonavir Form II

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:37.120
is substantially more stable than Form I.

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:39.440
And the way we know it's more stable

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:41.600
is because it's less soluble.

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:43.440
But if that crystal structure

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:46.720
happens to be much more stable,

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.680
then it won't dissolve properly.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:53.680
And then it's a bit like you
haven't taken the drug at all.

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:57.120
- But with chocolate, we can
change which polymorph we have.

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:00.880
We just had to heat it up to
switch it from shiny to dull,

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:03.680
and then by cooling it down
again in a specific way,

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:05.680
we could get back to shiny.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.440
So you might expect that Abbott

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.840
could just do something
similar with ritonavir.

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:12.400
And they tried, but the problem was

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:15.200
that no amount of heating or cooling

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.080
could turn Form II back into Form I.

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:19.440
They were stuck.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:24.400
We can see what's happening
by taking a look at this here.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:27.280
See, each polymorph has
different energy levels.

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:30.560
And in the case of chocolate
that looks something like this,

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:33.040
where Form IV has a higher energy level

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:34.960
and Form V a lower one,

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:38.080
and they're separated with
this sort of hill in between.

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:40.400
Now, after heating up the chocolate bar,

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:42.400
we were mostly left with Form IV.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:45.760
So let's drop this little ball in there,

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:49.120
and then you'll see it
will slowly settle down

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:51.040
into that valley.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.920
But not to the more stable Form V.

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:56.800
And that's because there's
this little hill in between.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:21:59.280
But now imagine adding some heat to this.

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:02.080
It's like giving the ball
a little bit of a kick.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:03.920
And you can see that the ball

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:05.920
will suddenly start to move around.

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:08.480
And if I give it enough of a kick,

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:11.840
whoop, it will roll down into Form V.

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:13.760
And now it is stuck there.

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:15.600
Now you could keep adding more heat

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:19.120
and you could get it back
over the hill back to Form IV,

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:23.440
but then you would just end
up with a mess of both forms,

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.680
because whenever you start
cooling it down again,

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:27.520
you know, the ball could
just randomly settle

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:28.720
in one of the two valleys.

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:29.440
So that's what happened

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:31.520
when we melted the
chocolate uncontrollably.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.720
We just got a mixture of these two forms.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:38.240
But with ritonavir, the
situation is a little different.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:41.200
The hill between the two
forms is now much taller,

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:44.320
but the Form II valley
is also much deeper.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.080
So once the ball does get down there,

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:49.200
it's basically impossible
to get it back out of there,

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:52.400
which is why no matter what
the scientists at Abbott tried,

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:55.760
they couldn't get back to Form I.

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.680
- But this still doesn't
explain why Form II

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:01.520
was suddenly everywhere.

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:03.840
Nothing had changed in their procedures.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:07.200
The barrier between the two
forms should still be there.

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:08.800
So it shouldn't have been possible

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:11.360
to make this much Form II at all.

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:13.600
And yet, 300 years earlier,

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.600
legends of such a transformation

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.224
spread across northern Europe.

00:23:18.224 --> 00:23:18.238
(gothic music)

00:23:18.238 --> 00:23:20.560
- It was a bitter winter morning,

00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:22.560
and it had been like this for months.

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:25.120
The organist was on
his way to a cathedral.

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:27.520
The cold had been messing
with the organ pipes.

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:29.360
It's gone out of tune again.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:32.160
But that wasn't what the
congregation thought.

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:34.400
There were stories of other organs

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:39.280
getting sick with warts or
leprosy eating away at the pipes.

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:42.080
Some thought it was the
devil attacking the organ

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:45.040
to punish an unfaithful flock.

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:48.080
It was even said that
when it was very quiet,

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:50.080
you could hear these organs screaming

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:54.720
and groaning in pain from the lesions.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.160
Nonsense, of course,

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:59.600
it was just the metal
contracting and expanding.

00:23:59.600 --> 00:23:59.760
(subdued music)

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:03.040
Except these pipes
weren't just contracting,

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:04.480
they were cracked.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:06.160
And others are indeed covered

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:08.800
in what looks like these lesions,

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:17.108
black growths all over the organ.

00:24:17.108 --> 00:24:17.120
(dark music)

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:18.880
Now, originally when this happened,

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:21.760
people thought that this
was the work of Satan.

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:23.360
Of course, that's not what was going on,

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:25.840
and we can explain what
was actually happening.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.160
So we've got some normal tin right here,

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:30.800
which is what those organ
pipes were made out of.

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:34.160
It kind of looks silver,
it feels pretty strong,

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:36.240
and it's sort of the form we're used to.

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:37.020
- Exactly.

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:40.640
- But here we have a slightly
different form of tin.

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:43.440
You can look at it, it's a bit more gray,

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:45.120
it's a bit more crumbly.

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.400
And at room temperature,

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:50.160
normally the silvery tin
is sort of more stable.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:53.040
But if you cool tin down

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:55.680
to something like below
13 degrees Celsius,

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.040
and ideally way colder,

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:59.840
then it can transform into
this new kind of gray tin.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:01.040
And we're gonna see what happens

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:04.464
when we put it on top of the silver tin.

00:25:04.464 --> 00:25:05.520
- [Dr. Kafizas] We want to try and get it

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.743
to around minus 30 degrees Celsius.

00:25:07.743 --> 00:25:07.758
- [Casper] Yep.

00:25:07.758 --> 00:25:09.760
- And what better to get
us to those temperatures

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.038
is dry ice.
- Okay.

00:25:11.038 --> 00:25:14.320
- So dry ice is frozen carbon dioxide.

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:17.567
And that is around minus
78 degrees Celsius.

00:25:17.567 --> 00:25:18.320
- [Casper] Yep.

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:20.320
Now we've taken a thermo flask

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:21.920
and filled it up with dry ice,

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:25.680
and then put a platform on top
on which we'll put our tin.

00:25:25.680 --> 00:25:29.840
This should cool it down to
around minus 30 degrees Celsius.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:32.640
Now we left this here for around 14 hours,

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:34.480
and what you'll see is that initially

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:36.720
there's a very tiny speck of tin

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.040
that suddenly transformed into gray tin,

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:40.880
and then it spreads from there

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:43.680
almost like an infectious disease,

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:47.200
which is why this is
also known as tin pest.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:50.400
And because gray tin is
less dense, the tin expands.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.240
And so if you look closely,

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:56.560
you can see it start to
tear apart the metal.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:59.040
Now, normally it takes a lot of energy

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:02.400
to transform some silver
tin into gray tin,

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.200
but once you get a tiny bit of gray tin,

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:06.400
something strange happens,

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:08.960
because now it acts as a nucleation site

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:10.720
that other tin can attach to,

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:13.600
and it effectively brings
that hill way down.

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:15.360
It lowers the activation energy.

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:16.960
And so now it becomes very easy

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:18.960
to switch from silver tin to gray tin.

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:23.120
And so it starts to spread,
it starts to take over.

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:25.680
And the same thing was
happening to those organ pipes.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:28.160
Once you got a lesion
on one of those pipes,

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:30.800
well then it would grow
and spread everywhere.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.880
Little flakes would come off the pipes

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:36.720
and seed all the others,
and it would spread.

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:40.080
And that's also exactly what
happened with ritonavir.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:42.560
Once a tiny bit of Form II appeared,

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:44.960
it acted as a nucleation site,

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:47.440
lowering that massive activation energy

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:51.840
and causing all the Form I
to crystallize into Form II.

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:54.560
Tiny seed crystals then broke off,

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:56.960
could become airborne, and spread,

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:59.280
attaching themselves to people's clothes

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:02.240
and making it to other parts
of the production line,

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:04.400
effectively seeding them

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:07.360
so that when new
ritonavir was synthesized,

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:09.200
it contained these seed crystals

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:12.800
and turned the entire
capsules into Form II.

00:27:12.800 --> 00:27:14.640
And because everyone likely had

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.720
these seed crystals on their clothes,

00:27:16.720 --> 00:27:19.840
when the Chicago team flew over to Italy,

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:22.560
they seeded that factory too.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:23.920
And in this way,

00:27:23.920 --> 00:27:27.678
soon not a single place was
able to manufacture Form I.

00:27:27.678 --> 00:27:31.520
- [Derek] Ritonavir is
arguably the most dramatic case

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:34.720
of what we now call a
disappearing polymorph.

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:37.840
The YouTube channel Reactions
made a great video about this

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:39.760
that involves lots of physical demos,

00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:42.160
so I highly recommend you check it out.

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.280
- [Narrator] "When this happened to us,

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:45.680
we conducted an extremely
thorough investigation

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:46.720
to see if there was something

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:49.040
that we did which would have caused this.

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:50.160
While we've speculated

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.480
on the cause of this
chemical transformation,

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:55.800
we do not have conclusive
proof of what happened."

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:59.120
- It might be that a mistake
on the production line

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:01.760
caused some chemicals to dry out.

00:28:01.760 --> 00:28:03.760
This might have created a new crystal

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:06.400
similar in shape to Form II ritonavir,

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:08.240
which acted like a seed.

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:10.560
Or it might have just been bad luck

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:13.880
that a seed crystal formed
on its own purely by chance.

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:16.480
- Even if you have a seed crystal,

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:18.640
if there are some dust particles

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:23.680
or some scratches in the recipient

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:28.000
where actually crystals
can start to nucleate,

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:30.880
that can induce, then,
different crystal structures.

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:34.640
So it happens that in some
pharmaceutical companies

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:39.360
where they produced the same
polymorph for years and years,

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:41.840
that suddenly there is, I would say,

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:44.960
a hair or some other particle that,

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:46.800
kind of, gets into the process

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:51.760
and will change the entire
crystallization of the compound,

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:54.240
and is then very difficult to control.

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:56.880
- And once a more stable
form has appeared,

00:28:56.880 --> 00:29:00.160
it can spread and quickly
seed the entire planet.

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:01.520
- It might be that you

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:04.320
will never, ever get the
initial polymorph again.

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:07.200
- After five months of
research, Abbott's researchers

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:10.692
held a press conference
to share their findings.

00:29:10.692 --> 00:29:10.707
(intense music)

00:29:10.707 --> 00:29:11.760
- [Narrator] "Good afternoon.

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:13.200
My colleagues and I are here today

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:16.640
to explain what has happened,
why it has happened,

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:18.240
how we've responded to the problem,

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:21.280
and what we're going to
do to correct the problem.

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:22.640
Sometime during this summer,

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:25.760
the semi-solid formulation of ritonavir

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:28.000
began to change into a crystal form,

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:29.600
a transformation that we believed

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:39.027
was a scientific and
chemical impossibility.

00:29:39.027 --> 00:29:41.680
- [Narrator] "You are a
large multinational company.

00:29:41.680 --> 00:29:44.000
Your scientists are obviously smart.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:45.600
How could this happen?"

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:48.720
- [Narrator] "A company's
size and the collective IQs

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:52.000
of their scientists have no
relationship to this problem.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.720
This phenomenon is, I
believe, unpredictable.

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:58.640
We are, in some sense,
the victim of bad luck.

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:01.280
There are many mysteries of
nature that we've not solved.

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:02.480
Hurricanes, for example,

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:05.680
continue to occur and often
cause massive devastation.

00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:07.360
There is nothing that we can do today

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:09.920
to prevent a hurricane
from striking any community

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:13.120
or polymorphism from striking any drug.

00:30:13.120 --> 00:30:20.543
Science cannot provide a
solution to all our problems."

00:30:20.543 --> 00:30:20.558
(curious music)

00:30:20.558 --> 00:30:21.920
- Now, here's a good question.

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.400
Is everything polymorphic?

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:28.800
So nobody had discovered a
polymorph of aspirin, right?

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:31.200
So it had been around, it's
one of the earliest drugs.

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:33.920
It had been crystallized in industry

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:38.080
for what, 130, 140 years?

00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:39.680
And so can you say,

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:41.440
"Because nobody had discovered

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:43.360
a polymorph of aspirin, therefore..."

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:44.640
No. Right?

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:46.080
The only problem is

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:50.320
I discovered Form II of aspirin. (laughs)

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:51.520
By accident. Right?

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:54.400
- It turns out over half of all compounds

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:57.040
are known to be polymorphic,
and there could be more.

00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:58.560
- The number of polymorphs

00:30:58.560 --> 00:31:00.400
is proportional to the amount of time

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:03.360
and money you spend
researching that compound.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:04.880
- In fact, nowadays we know

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:08.720
there are not two forms of
ritonavir, but at least five.

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:11.440
So are new cases of
disappearing polymorphs

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:12.560
something to worry about?

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:14.880
- It's quite, quite rare.

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:17.520
We certainly know a lot more

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:21.680
than we did when ritonavir occurred,

00:31:21.680 --> 00:31:25.440
but I wouldn't be surprised
to see it happen again.

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:28.240
- If there's a 1% chance
the world's gonna end,

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:30.720
you're gonna do something about it, right?

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:33.040
If there's a 1% chance
a plane is gonna crash,

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:36.080
you're not gonna fly, right?

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:37.760
So, so yeah.

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:42.720
So we're at that situation
where it might only be

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:45.760
in that order of 1%, but if it happens,

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:47.520
it's gonna cost you a hell of a lot more

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:50.080
than a few weeks of
research on polymorphs.

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:55.040
Ritonavir was one of the red flags

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:58.080
that caused a lot of regulatory activity

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:01.800
and a lot of scientific
activity around polymorphs.

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:03.920
- Nowadays, pharmaceutical companies

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:05.440
can spend hundreds of thousands

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:08.560
to millions of dollars
screening for polymorphs.

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:10.160
In the end, there was no way

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:12.960
of getting Form I ritonavir
back successfully.

00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:16.160
There were attempts, but
all were incredibly costly,

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:19.120
and they risked being infected again.

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:20.960
So instead, Abbott went back

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:23.280
to an older liquid formulation of the drug

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:26.467
and abandoned Form I entirely.

00:32:26.467 --> 00:32:28.240
- [Narrator] "Our initial
activities were directed

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:30.960
towards eliminating Form
II from our environment.

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:33.440
We finally accepted that we could not.

00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:35.440
Our subsequent activities were directed

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:39.280
towards figuring out how
to live in a Form II world.

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:43.583
Nature would appear to favor it."

00:32:43.583 --> 00:32:43.598
(curious music)

00:32:43.598 --> 00:32:46.000
- The liquid formulation was not ideal.

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:47.520
It had worse side effects,

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:51.587
and not all patients could
tolerate it well, but it worked.

00:32:51.587 --> 00:32:53.040
- [Narrator] "It is frightening
that this could happen

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:55.920
to any drug that we've taken
and on which we're dependent,

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:57.920
even though it is not that common.

00:32:57.920 --> 00:32:59.600
This time, it has happened to Abbott

00:32:59.600 --> 00:33:01.520
and to the tens of thousands of people

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:03.520
taking the semi-solid capsule.

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:06.480
Thankfully, we had the liquid
formulation as a safety net.

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:08.640
Next time it may happen to another drug

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:13.200
that may not have the safety net."

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:14.877
(curious music continues)

